          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of William Faulkner</strong><br>\nWilliam Faulkner was born in 1897 in New Albany, Mississippi. He was the first of four sons. Growing up in the South, his family moved to Oxford, Mississippi, where he spent most of his childhood. Faulkner was influenced by the region's rich history and storytelling traditions. He did not complete high school and dropped out in the 11th grade, but his passion for writing was evident early on.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginning of Faulkner's Writing Career</strong><br>\nIn the early 1920s, Faulkner began to take writing seriously. He published his first book, \"The Marble Faun,\" a collection of poetry, in 1924. Despite the initial lack of success, he was determined to become a writer. Faulkner's early works were influenced by his Southern background and the complex history of his home region.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Faulkner's Literary Style</strong><br>\nFaulkner's writing style is known for its intricate narratives and complex character psychology. He often used stream-of-consciousness narration and multiple viewpoints. This style became a hallmark of his novels, giving readers a deep insight into his characters' thoughts and motivations. His exploration of Southern life was both critical and sympathetic, showing the struggles of individuals against a background of decline and change.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
