          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Critical Reception and Struggles</strong><br>\nFaulkner's early literary works were not immediately successful. He struggled to gain recognition and worked various jobs to support himself while writing. However, he continued to develop his distinctive narrative techniques, which would later be celebrated. His novel \"Sartoris,\" published in 1929, was the first to gain significant attention, setting the stage for his future success.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Breakthrough with \"The Sound and the Fury\"</strong><br>\nThe Sound and the Fury, published in 1929, was Faulkner's breakthrough. The novel's innovative structure and style were initially confusing to readers, but it has since become a classic, praised for its bold experimentation with narrative form. It reflects Faulkner's interest in the themes of time and history, which would recur throughout his works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Depiction of the American South</strong><br>\nFaulkner's novels often depict the American South and its history. He created the fictional Yoknapatawpha County, based on his own experiences in Mississippi. This setting was used in many of his novels, including \"Light in August\" and \"Absalom, Absalom!\" Through these stories, Faulkner examined the social, economic, and racial issues of the South.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
