          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1949, Faulkner was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature. The Nobel committee praised his powerful and artistically unique contribution to the modern American novel. Faulkner's acceptance speech emphasized the human spirit and the writer's duty to write about the problems of the human heart in conflict with itself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Works and Their Impact</strong><br>\nFaulkner continued to write until his death in 1962. His later works, like \"A Fable\" and \"The Reivers,\" which won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction, further cemented his legacy. Faulkner's influence extends beyond literature; he is regarded as one of the most important writers in American history, impacting countless authors and readers worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Faulkner's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nWilliam Faulkner died on July 6, 1962, in Byhalia, Mississippi. His legacy endures through his profound impact on literature and his portrayal of Southern life. Faulkner's work continues to be studied for its artistic depth and portrayal of universal human themes, earning him a permanent place in the canon of world literature.</p>\n"
              },
